Subject: SUMMARY: specs for test Alpha machine
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078617110484

   I asked about recommendations for a smaller (than our production
2100A and 4100) Alpha box that could dual boot to DU and Windows NT and
have at least 18G .  Thanks very much to Paul Henderson, Paul Kitwin,
Donald Rye, Alan, Randy Perry, and especially Rod McMillan (who was nice
enough to phone with his thoughts when his email bounced).  Apologies to
anyone else that happened to, the day after I posted our email gods
changed my address and while they claimed forwarding would work it took
quite a while before it did.  I hadn't realized they were about to do
that or would have posted later!  

These are the recommended systems and configurations and the advantages
mentioned:

Alpha 1000A:  much better than 800, cheaper than 2100, lots of RAM that
you can buy from 3rd parties, lots of slots, lots of hard drive space
Alpha 300 and 800 also possibilities:  " Depending on how you connect
the storage, 18 GB is trivial (two 9 GB disks).  The 300, 800, and 1000A
each have PCI expansion slots so an extra SCSI adapter is easy.  All are
available with licenses for each operating system, so they'll all run
all three.  I think all three
have both consoles available."

Alpha station 255:  "Add a single channel RAID controller (KZPSC-AA?)
and a BA350 (Storage
works box)  with 5 each 4 Gb Drives (RZ29B-VA) and have two 2 Gb Drives
(RZ28). You can have NT on one of the 2 Gb drives and UNIX on the other.
The dual boot would be a simple shutdown, physically swap the drive, and
boot."  Reason the dual boot would work this way is that with Alpha 255
systems there is only "enough console memory for one or the other
console; SRM or ARC.  To switch
to NT from Digital UNIX or VMS and back requires loading the other
console."

Digital 433au workstations:  "These have a firmware revision that allows
you to boot EITHER DU or NT with no firmware changes! Just install DU on
one disk, NT on another, select the disk to boot from, and you've
changed OS's.  They are relatively inexpensive, and you can get
StorageWorks expansion boxes for the additional storage you need."  Or
can "Hook up a simple raid controller and you are ready to go."     
These au workstations also come in 500 and 600mhz besides 433.

Obviously these are several different classes of system but the
information is very helpful.  This got back-burnered at our site for
awhile and I haven't researched further; if I find out more later I'll
post a second summary.

						Anne Peticolas
						Austin Automation Center
